1️⃣ What is Routing?

Suppose computer A wants to send data to computer B.

There may be many possible paths:

R2
      /  \
A ─ R1    R4 ─ B
      \  /
       R3

The router must decide:

> Which path should the packet take?



This process is called routing.


---

2️⃣ Routing vs Forwarding

Routing

Determines the best path.

Forwarding

Actually sends the packet through the selected interface.

Routing
   ↓
"Which path?"

Forwarding
   ↓
"Send it this way →"


---

3️⃣ Routing Table

A router maintains a routing table.

Example:

Destination	Next Hop	Interface

192.168.1.0/24	Direct	eth0
10.0.0.0/8	R2	eth1
172.16.0.0/16	R3	eth2


When a packet arrives:

Destination IP
      ↓
Routing Table
      ↓
Find best match
      ↓
Forward packet


---

🔥 4️⃣ Longest Prefix Matching

This is very important.

Suppose a router has:

10.0.0.0/8
10.1.0.0/16
10.1.1.0/24

Destination:

10.1.1.25

It matches all three.

Which route is selected?

The route with the longest prefix:

\[
\boxed{10.1.1.0/24}
\]

Why?

Because /24 is more specific than /16 or /8.


---

5️⃣ Example

Routing table:

192.168.0.0/16 → R1
192.168.10.0/24 → R2
192.168.10.128/25 → R3

Destination:

192.168.10.150

It matches:

/16
/24
/25

Longest prefix:

\[
\boxed{/25}
\]

Therefore:

Next hop → R3


